S5 §11.8 (2) — ANCHORLESS FIXTURE CAUSALITY, RE-CAPTURED
========================================================

Claim under test (S5_REPORT.md §11.7 / §11.8 item 2): the single
`duplicate_sequence` drop that reddened
`wasm_anchorless::two_leaves_reach_one_direct_session_through_a_carrier_that_relays_no_packets`
at `72c8abd6b` was caused by the FIXTURE, not by production — its
inbound sink only QUEUED arriving datagrams and delivered them on the
next 50 ms tick, while `DEFAULT_RTO` is also 50 ms, so every reliable
packet was retransmitted once and the retransmit arrived as a
duplicate sequence. The bindgen surface's own sink delivers on
arrival for exactly this reason.

The traces the original claim rested on were not kept. This file is
the re-capture: the bounded inverse that puts the fixture back on the
slower wire it had invented, the exact command, its exit code, the
verbatim failure, and the restored run.

Host: Windows 11 Pro, local. Repository HEAD at capture:
477bc345e31bd1076200d17d1b1f2bf9fe2568a4
(working tree carries this round's in-flight repairs; the file under
mutation, `net/crates/net/leaf/tests/wasm_anchorless.rs`, was clean
before and after — `git status --porcelain` for that path is empty in
both places, shown below.)

EXACT COMMAND (identical for both runs, from `net/crates/net/leaf`):

  CHROMEDRIVER="<repo>/.tools/cd/chromedriver-win64/chromedriver.exe" \
  CARGO_TARGET_WASM32_UNKNOWN_UNKNOWN_RUNNER=wasm-bindgen-test-runner \
  WASM_BINDGEN_TEST_TIMEOUT=120 \
  cargo test --target wasm32-unknown-unknown \
      --features mock-control-plane --test wasm_anchorless

WHAT THE INVERSE ESTABLISHES

  restored source -> exit 0, 2 passed, 0 failed
  inverse source  -> exit 1, `duplicate_sequence` A=1 B=3,
                     `left: (1, 3)  right: (0, 0)`

The drop counter that fires under the inverse is
`duplicate_sequence`, at both ends, and nothing else moves. That is
the named mechanism, reproduced on demand, and it is the whole of the
causality claim: the fixture's delivery schedule produced the
retransmit; production's does not.

Note on the numbers: the original report described ONE
`duplicate_sequence` drop. The assertion was subsequently
strengthened from A-only to `(A, B) == (0, 0)`, so the inverse now
exposes B's three as well — the second retransmit cause the A-only
assertion could not see. The strengthening is why the figure differs
from the prose in §11.7, not a change in mechanism.


